,"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4018049","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cc6b5925cfe1de41fbeda01ec2b47589d910119413a56f8d18645cbe5e90d293","compact":"773ecf6ab6f9de82d5e41ce173efcebb51240da2aca1e7bc6e8fedf8b0614f80","nasal":"6ced2ec8cbea8969321d0aadfadd33679362ab8032a8a00723d260bcaf795f54"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-18-50","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":18,"verse":50,"english":"It behoveth you to so arrange as Sugrīva may not disregard that chaste Tārā who is blameable for my folly only.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-18-46"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4018050","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d9d13339ddd1668950c1aae8698f360e2317ceba9eab431c1cc07d694cfddcca","compact":"b21219c1b356f5d09d5f8359272906ec2c4c6cd21a47737d8e831c6a78557b00","nasal":"b21219c1b356f5d09d5f8359272906ec2c4c6cd21a47737d8e831c6a78557b00"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-18-51","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":18,"verse":51,"english":"He, who is favoured by you, abideth by yourcommandments and acteth after your heart, can acquire kingdom, attain to heaven, and govern the earth.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.875,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-18-47"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4018051","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0054b46126316b76d494f3eb0c448781160c7e790bd13ed8fadd86ea2388b56c","compact":"3a760e2be85206f1b022f629701de1933b06216de9023564ce98b5efc70ead4e","nasal":"3a760e2be85206f1b022f629701de1933b06216de9023564ce98b5efc70ead4e"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-18-52","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":18,"verse":52,"english":"Having addressed Rāma thus the lord of monkeys stopped. Thereupon Rāma consoled Vāli, of clear understanding with the following moral words, acceptable to the Saints. O best of monkeys, do you not consider ourselves as well as yourself blameable. We are more conversant, than you, with the principles of religion.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.964,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-18-49"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4018052","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0f521e6e1fc7ce8444e7047afc532c0c29cfd2161128ad4eeffc2b38c5674a48","compact":"a72fd8b1f5f530b7b0eb261ea54f32f0de4cc82c5ca408f0a31936fdf9e80289","nasal":"a72fd8b1f5f530b7b0eb261ea54f32f0de4cc82c5ca408f0a31936fdf9e80289"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-18-53","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":18,"verse":53,"english":"Having addressed Rāma thus the lord of monkeys stopped. Thereupon Rāma consoled Vāli, of clear understanding with the following moral words, acceptable to the Saints. O best of monkeys, do you not consider ourselves as well as yourself blameable. We are more conversant, than you, with the principles of religion.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-18-49"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4018053","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"049ac09747e217a611c66c00ac5d1a3abde8898613c348de5e6c3846c25c2bd0","compact":"4ef863f135979b12e4a5f53e91f2ef18fa688a896f9c75d4673d54f42dd09512","nasal":"b21b11c53375827afef498711bd9e3844c59db87f7cc76830e0664341357ba2f"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-18-54","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":18,"verse":54,"english":"They never lose their virtue being proficient in the mode of ascerting crime and punishing it one administering punishment to the criminal and the criminal receiving it. And therefore receiving punishment (from me) you had been released from your sins and acquired a knowledge of religion.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"me